<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Br. J. Cancer</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10206318</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2362773</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6690309</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.bjc.6690309</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Epidemiology of Kaposi's sarcoma in Scotland, 1976&#x02013;1996</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Brewster</surname><given-names>D H</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Harris</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Black</surname><given-names>R J</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Goldberg</surname><given-names>D J</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1">Scottish Cancer Intelligence Unit, Information &#x00026; Statistics Division, Trinity Park House, Edinburgh, Scotland, EH5 3SQ, UK</aff><aff id="aff2">Scottish Centre for Infection and Environmental Health, Clifton House, Clifton Place, Glasgow, Scotland, G3 7LN, UK</aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>4</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>79</volume><issue>11-12</issue><fpage>1938</fpage><lpage>1942</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>17</day><month>09</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>22</day><month>10</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>28</day><month>10</month><year>1998</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1999</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>Based on data from the Scottish Cancer Registry, the epidemiology of Kaposi's sarcoma (KS) in Scotland during the period 1976&#x02013;96 is described. In males, the annual age-standardized incidence rate (World standard population) increased from less than 0.09 per 100 000 before 1986 to 0.44 in 1991 and then decreased to around 0.17. Peak incidence is now at ages 30&#x02013;39 compared with ages 80+ during the period 1976&#x02013;82; and by 1986&#x02013;96 the standardized incidence ratio for the Health Board which includes Edinburgh had risen to almost four times the national level. These changes are largely consistent with the pattern of HIV infection in Scotland. However, in both sexes, relative to other neoplasms, and in international terms, KS remains rare in Scotland. For patients diagnosed during 1976&#x02013;92, cumulative observed survival at 5 years was 8.7&#x00025; at ages 0&#x02013;49 compared with 49.8&#x00025; at ages 50&#x02013;84, reflecting the more aggressive course of AIDS-related KS, as well as the co-morbidity and competing causes of death associated with AIDS. &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>AIDS</kwd><kwd>epidemiology</kwd><kwd>incidence</kwd><kwd>Kaposi's sarcoma</kwd><kwd>Scotland</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>